    "result_text": "All tests pass. The change is complete.\n\n## Summary\n\n`namesakeCount` is now assigned at create time (a snapshot of how many existing owners share the same first+last name, case-insensitively) and returned on the owner \u2014 matching the codebase's existing pattern of derived attributes (`customerCode`, `householdId`).\n\n**Files changed:**\n\n1. **`AssignOwnerNamesakeCount.java`** (new) \u2014 a small, self-contained pipeline step that counts existing owners with the same first/last name (case-insensitively) and stores it on the not-yet-saved owner. WMC_other = 0, so it carries no surrounding complexity.\n\n2. **`owners.POST.yml`** \u2014 wired the new `countNamesakes` step between `assignCustomerCode` and `save`, so the count excludes the owner being created.\n\n3. **`Owner.java`** \u2014 added the `namesakeCount` field with a plain getter/setter (each CC 1).\n\n4. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 added the read-only `namesakeCount` property to the `Owner` schema; the generated `OwnerDto` picks it up and MapStruct maps it by name automatically (no mapper edit needed), so both the GET and create responses include it.\n\n5. **DB schema** (`hsqldb`, `h2`, `mysql`, `postgres` `schema.sql`) \u2014 added the `namesake_count` column, consistent with how `household_id`/`customer_code` exist across all four.\n\n6. **Seed data** (`hsqldb`, `mysql` `data.sql`) \u2014 appended a trailing `NULL` to the positional owner inserts. The h2 and postgres seed data use explicit column lists, so they needed no change.\n\nI kept the code cost low by adding behaviour as one new cohesive function class rather than growing an existing one, touching `Owner` only with trivial accessors, and letting the mapper wire the DTO field by convention.",
